-## 4. Reading the logs
+## 4. Keeping the drift mean honest
+
+### Two consumers, opposite requirements
+
+`mcu_drifts[]` is a rolling window of the last `N_DRIFTS` (20) drift measurements. Upstream
+serves two consumers from it, both unfiltered, and they want opposite things.
+
+`log_drift_stats()` derives `drift_thres` from the q90 of that window. It is an **outlier
+detector**: it has to see outliers to stay calibrated against them.
+
+`sum_mcu_drifts`, the plain mean of the same window, is a **drift estimator**: it is meant to
+describe what the hardware is doing. It feeds three things:
+
+- the `Mean MCU drift vs SX130X#0` log line;
+- the `rt_utcOffset` drift-steering correction, when there is no PPS and no UTC anchor (see
+  [utc-anchor.md](utc-anchor.md));
+- `ts_normalizeTimespanMCU()`, which scales the `RefTime` published with every uplink.
+
+Outliers have no business in the second. A field unit logging
+`Mean MCU drift vs SX130X#0: -914ppm` during a startup lockup was reporting its own pairing
+error, not its TCXO - and `RefTime` was being scaled by that figure: 0.09 % of the interval
+since the last `MuxTime` at -914 ppm, 2.47 % at the +24 740 ppm seen on another gateway.
+
+### The obvious fix is wrong
+
+Moving the recording past the excessive-drift test, so that only accepted samples are ever
+stored, looks correct and is not. It is written down here precisely because it looks correct.
+
+Truncating the input to an outlier detector makes it **ratchet**. An accepted sample
+satisfies `|drift| <= drift_thres` by construction, so the quantile of a window built only
+from accepted samples is always at or below the `drift_thres` in force, and the next
+threshold derived from it is therefore always at or below the previous one. It converges on
+`MIN_MCU_DRIFT_THRES`, 2 ppm, regardless of what the hardware is doing.
+
+That variant was implemented and measured on target rather than reasoned about. Over 1 h 52
+in steady state:
+
+| | Baseline | Truncated collection |
+|---|---|---|
+| `Repeated excessive` warnings | 11.7/h | 34.8/h, 65 in total |
+
+It fired on perfectly ordinary drift - `20.5ppm (threshold 10.5ppm)`,
+`17.6ppm (threshold 10.5ppm)`, `13.8ppm (threshold 13.0ppm)` - while `drift_thres` sawtoothed
+downwards over about a dozen cycles, 39, 23, 17.6, 15.2, 13.3, 10.0 ppm, until the
+`excessive_drift_cnt >= 2*QUICK_RETRIES` reset pushed it back to `MAX_MCU_DRIFT_THRES` and
+the descent started again. One such cycle logged `threshold 15.7ppm` at 3 and 6 retries, then
+`threshold 100.0ppm` at 9. The variant was rejected on those numbers.
+
+### Filter at the point of use, not at the point of collection
+
+`mcu_drifts[]` keeps receiving every sample that reaches it, exactly as upstream, so the
+quantile stays calibrated on the full distribution it expects. Once `drift_thres` has been
+recomputed from that untouched window, the same window is walked again and only the entries
+within the threshold are averaged.
+
+One recording, two views: the detector sees everything, the estimator sees only what survived
+it. Neither compromises the other.
+
+`mean_mcu_drift` replaces `sum_mcu_drifts` and its per-call incremental maintenance, which is
+no longer needed: the truncated mean is computed fresh each time `drift_thres` is recomputed,
+once per completed window. It feeds the same three consumers.
+
+### The empty-set case
+
+If every entry in the window exceeds the just-computed `drift_thres`, the truncated set is
+empty and no mean can be derived from it. This is reachable: `drift_thres` is clamped to at
+most `MAX_MCU_DRIFT_THRES`, 100 ppm, and during a lockup every recorded value can sit far
+above that.
+
+The previous `mean_mcu_drift` is then kept rather than replaced, which matches how the log
+line and the `rt_utcOffset` correction already behave before the first window ever completes.
+`ts_iniTimesync()` resets it to 0, like `sum_mcu_drifts` before it: `decodeDriftPPM(0)` is
+1.0, that is, no correction.
+
+### Upstream scope kept, deliberately
+
+Two oddities are preserved rather than fixed, to keep the change to what it claims to be:
+
+- the truncated mean is only assigned under `txunit == 0`, mirroring where `sum_mcu_drifts`
+  was maintained - even though the surrounding `widx == 0` block runs for every txunit, so
+  its `Mean MCU drift vs SX130X#0` line and its `rt_utcOffset` correction still read
+  txunit 0's figures whichever txunit triggered the block;
+- the second, unreachable `return` in `ts_normalizeTimespanMCU()` is left in place; only the
+  identifier it references changes.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Reading the logs
 
 Module `SYN`. The existing warning, emitted every `QUICK_RETRIES` rejections, gains two
